.Dd April 18, 2026
.Dt TINC-AUTH 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tinc-auth
.Nd nginx auth_request backend and OIDC provider for tinc node identity
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl n Ar NETNAME
.Op Fl c Ar DIR
.Op Fl -pidfile Ns = Ns Ar FILE
.Op Fl -listen-socket Ns = Ns Ar PATH
.Op Fl -idp-listen Ns = Ns Ar ADDR:PORT
.Op Fl -issuer Ns = Ns Ar URL
.Op Fl -clients Ns = Ns Ar FILE
.Op Fl -groups Ns = Ns Ar FILE
.Op Fl -email-domain Ns = Ns Ar DOMAIN
.Op Fl -id-token-ttl Ns = Ns Ar DURATION
.Op Fl -access-token-ttl Ns = Ns Ar DURATION
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
listens on a Unix-domain socket and answers
.Xr nginx 8
.Sy auth_request
sub-requests.
For each request it reads the
.Sy Remote-Addr
header (set to
.Va $remote_addr
by the nginx configuration), connects to the local
.Xr tincd 8
control socket, and performs a longest-prefix match against the
running daemon's subnet table.
.Pp
The listening socket is taken from systemd socket activation
.Pq Ev LISTEN_FDS , exactly one stream socket
when available, otherwise from
.Fl -listen-socket .
The
.Xr tincd 8
control socket is located via
.Fl -pidfile
or
.Fl n Ns \&/ Ns Fl c ,
exactly as for
.Xr tinc 8 .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl n , Fl -net Ns = Ns Ar NETNAME
Network name; also reported in the
.Sy Tinc-Net
header.
.It Fl c , Fl -config Ns = Ns Ar DIR
Configuration directory.
.It Fl -pidfile Ns = Ns Ar FILE
Path to
.Xr tincd 8 Ap s
pidfile (the control socket path is derived from it).
.It Fl -listen-socket Ns = Ns Ar PATH , Fl -sockpath Ns = Ns Ar PATH
Unix socket to accept nginx sub-requests on.
Ignored under socket activation.
.It Fl -idp-listen Ns = Ns Ar ADDR:PORT
Serve an OIDC provider on this TCP address.
The address must fall inside a subnet owned by this node,
otherwise
.Nm
refuses to start.
See
.Sx OIDC PROVIDER .
.It Fl -issuer Ns = Ns Ar URL
OIDC issuer URL as relying parties should see it,
typically
.Li http:// Ns Ar ADDR:PORT
of
.Fl -idp-listen .
Required with
.Fl -idp-listen .
.It Fl -clients Ns = Ns Ar FILE
JSON array of relying parties:
.Li id ,
.Li secret
and
.Li redirect_uris
(matched exactly).
Required with
.Fl -idp-listen .
.It Fl -groups Ns = Ns Ar FILE
JSON object mapping node names to group lists,
emitted as the
.Li groups
claim.
Unlisted nodes get an empty list.
.It Fl -email-domain Ns = Ns Ar DOMAIN
Emit an
.Li email
claim of the form
.Ar node Ns @ Ns Ar DOMAIN .
Off by default because the address is synthetic.
.It Fl -id-token-ttl Ns = Ns Ar DURATION , Fl -access-token-ttl Ns = Ns Ar DURATION
Token lifetimes, e.g.
.Li 5m
or
.Li 1h .
Defaults: 5m and 1h.
.It Fl -help , Fl -version
Print help or version and exit.
.El
.Sh RESPONSES
.Bl -tag -width "503 Service Unavailable" -compact
.It Sy 204 \&No Content
.Sy Remote-Addr
matched a tinc subnet.
The response carries:
.Bl -tag -width "Tinc-Subnet" -offset indent -compact
.It Sy Tinc-Node
Owning node name.
.It Sy Tinc-Net
.Ar NETNAME
(empty if none was given).
.It Sy Tinc-Subnet
The matching subnet.
.El
.It Sy 401 Unauthorized
No subnet contains the address, or
.Sy Remote-Addr
did not parse as an IP address.
.It Sy 400 Bad Request
.Sy Remote-Addr
header missing, or the request was malformed.
.It Sy 503 Service Unavailable
.Xr tincd 8
is not running or its control socket is unreachable.
.El
.Pp
.Sy (broadcast)
subnets are never reported as a match.
.Sh OIDC PROVIDER
With
.Fl -idp-listen ,
.Nm
also serves an OpenID Connect provider for applications that speak
OIDC natively (Gitea, Grafana, ...).
There is no login page: a browser visiting the authorization
endpoint from a mesh address is identified by that address, exactly
like the
.Sy auth_request
path.
The endpoints are
.Li /.well-known/openid-configuration ,
.Li /.well-known/jwks.json ,
.Li /authorize ,
.Li /token
and
.Li /userinfo .
.Pp
ID tokens are RS256-signed JWTs with claims
.Li sub
and
.Li preferred_username
(the node name),
.Li tinc_net ,
.Li tinc_subnet ,
.Li groups
and optionally
.Li email .
The signing key is generated on first start into
.Pa CONFDIR/idp/oidc-key.pem .
Authorization codes support PKCE
.Pq S256 .
Refresh tokens are not issued.
.Pp
The provider serves plain HTTP.
The transport is the mesh, which tinc already encrypts and
authenticates, so relying parties must be configured to accept an
http issuer and must reach the provider over the mesh.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
location = /_tinc_auth {
    internal;
    proxy_pass http://unix:/run/tinc-auth.sock;
    proxy_pass_request_body off;
    proxy_set_header Remote-Addr $remote_addr;
}
location / {
    auth_request /_tinc_auth;
    auth_request_set $tinc_node $upstream_http_tinc_node;
    proxy_set_header X-Tinc-Node $tinc_node;
    proxy_pass http://backend;
}
.Ed
.Pp
OIDC provider on the node's mesh address:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tinc-auth -n mesh --pidfile /run/tinc.mesh.pid \e
    --idp-listen 10.20.0.2:8443 \e
    --issuer http://10.20.0.2:8443 \e
    --clients /etc/tinc-auth/clients.json
.Ed
.Pp
with
.Pa clients.json :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[{"id": "gitea", "secret": "...",
  "redirect_uris": ["http://git.mesh/user/oauth2/tinc/callback"]}]
.Ed
.Pp
A full walkthrough for both modes is in
.Pa docs/AUTH.md .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nginx 8 ,
.Xr tinc 8 ,
.Xr tincd 8
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
authenticates the tinc
.Em node ,
not a human user.
Any process on a node that owns a matching subnet appears as that node.
